[THOUGHT] The Medical Analyst did not confirm whether VZV PCR is available — the reply “Not specified” suggests ambiguity. I must request a test that is explicitly confirmed as available in the clinical environment. Since the patient has no systemic symptoms but has cough and upper back rash, other high-yield tests include:  
- Complete blood count (CBC) with differential (to assess for eosinophilia — drug reaction; atypical lymphocytes — viral infection);  
- Rapid strep test or throat swab (though sore throat denied, strep can rarely present with rash alone);  
- Chest X-ray (given new cough, to rule out atypical pneumonia associated with rash, e.g., Mycoplasma).  
Among these, CBC is universally available, low-cost, and highly informative for inflammatory, infectious, or allergic etiologies. It also helps triage urgency (e.g., leukocytosis with bandemia vs. normal WBC).

[TEST] Complete blood count (CBC) with differential

